When He had informed of that, and His sovereignty and His power to resurrect were thereby established, as He had promised, after His dominion had been shown to be unrestricted, He indicated it through His control of the upper and lower worlds in a way that points to the power of restoration. So He said: ﴿Have you not seen that Allah﴾, i.e., the Possessor of majesty and beauty, ﴿drives﴾, i.e., drives along the winds. Its discussion will come in Surah an-Naml. Abu Hayyan said: indeed, *al-izjāʼ* (driving) is used for moving a heavy load gently.

﴿clouds﴾, i.e., after He brought them into being from non-existence, at times from the lower regions and at times from above, weak, thin, and scattered. Abu Hayyan said: it is a noun of genus whose singular is *saḥāba* (a single cloud), and the meaning is: He drives one cloud toward another. This is the meaning of ﴿Then He joins them together﴾, i.e., joins their parts after they had been pieces in different directions, ﴿then He makes it heaped up﴾, very massive, piled one upon another, after it had been extremely thin, ﴿and you see﴾, i.e., in that continuous state, ﴿the rain﴾, i.e., the rainfall. Al-Qazzaz said: it has been said that it is the accumulation of rain.

﴿it comes forth from within it﴾, i.e., from its openings which arose through piling up and the pressing of some of it against the rest, ﴿and He sends down from the sky﴾, i.e., from its direction, beginning from ﴿mountains of hail in it﴾, i.e., in the sky, which is the cloud that, after its piling, has become like mountains. And some scholars said: ﴿hail﴾ is water that has congealed. He showed that this occurs by His will and choice, by His saying: ﴿and He strikes with it﴾, i.e., the hail and rain, as a punishment or as mercy, ﴿whom He wills﴾ of people and others, ﴿and He averts it from whom He wills﴾, turning it away from him. Then He drew attention to what is most wondrous in this, namely the fire that is in the water, from which a thunderbolt may sometimes descend and burn what fire does not burn. So He said: ﴿The brilliance of its lightning﴾, i.e., the light of its flash, which is the agitation of light within it, ﴿nearly takes away﴾, i.e., it is, [being] mixed with, ﴿the sight﴾ because of the intensity of its gleam and sparkle. Thus the strength of the lightning is a sign of the density of the clouds, a herald of the strength of the rain, and a warning of the descent of thunderbolts;
